# Always commit and push

After you finish work that changes the repo (code, config, migrations, scripts, plans, rules), **commit and push** before ending the turn. Do not wait to be asked.

## Workflow

1. Run `git status` and `git diff` to see what changed.
2. Stage only files related to the task (include `.cursor/plans/` and `.cursor/rules/` created for the task).
3. Never stage secrets (`.env`, `.env.*`, credentials, API keys) or unrelated local dumps.
4. Write a concise commit message focused on why (1 sentence, imperative mood).
5. Commit, then `git push` to the current branch (`git push -u origin HEAD` if no upstream).
6. If the work should go live on production, also merge into `main` and `git push -u origin main` (do not force-push).
7. Confirm the commit hash and branch in your reply.

## Do not commit

- `.env`, `.env.*`, credential files, diagnostic dumps (`diagnostic-report.*`), or other local-only artifacts unless the user explicitly asks.
- Unrelated untracked files from other work.

## If nothing to commit

Say so briefly. Do not create empty commits.

## If push fails

Report the error and what the user needs to do (auth, conflicts, etc.). Do not force-push unless explicitly requested.
